---
// Home — Harbor Ridge V1, Workstream F Step 3.
// Content status: PROVISIONAL IMPLEMENTATION COPY — NOT FROZEN.
// Text is verbatim from docs/harbor-ridge-workstream-f-step3-home-page-copy.md;
// only structural/presentational markup (headings, lists, Wordmark treatment,
// video-placeholder regions, link styling) has been added. Do not reword.
import Layout from '../components/Layout.astro';
import Wordmark from '../components/Wordmark.astro';
---

<Layout title="Ken Corioso — HEOS Evidence Engine | Harbor Ridge V1">
  <main class="home">
    <!-- Block A — Hero -->
    <section class="block block--hero">
      <div class="measure-display">
        <h1 class="hero-headline">
          Healthcare Growth Problems Rarely Live in One Department. I Built an
          AI System to See Across Them.
        </h1>
        <p class="lead">
          I built the <Wordmark name="HEOS Evidence Engine" /> to connect marketing,
          admissions, treatment, and revenue into one evidence chain, helping
          executives investigate performance across organizational silos and
          inspect the evidence behind each conclusion. Then I subjected its AI
          reasoning to a controlled blind evaluation.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Block B — The Problem -->
    <section class="block">
      <div class="measure">
        <h2 class="section-heading">The Problem</h2>
        <p>
          Harbor Ridge Behavioral Health started with a disagreement
          healthcare executives know well: completed admissions and census
          were declining even as marketing spend and inquiry volume were
          going up. Marketing believed admissions wasn't converting the
          demand being generated. Admissions believed marketing was sending
          lower-quality leads. Neither side had the evidence to settle it.
        </p>
        <p>
          I didn't want to optimize one department's version of the story. I
          wanted to investigate the whole chain.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Block C — What I Built -->
    <section class="block">
      <div class="measure">
        <h2 class="section-heading">What I Built</h2>
        <p>
          That AI system — the <Wordmark name="HEOS Evidence Engine" /> — needed
          a real test, not just a demo. So I built Harbor Ridge, a fictional,
          entirely synthetic 32-bed behavioral health facility, and connected
          its full operating chain: <strong>acquisition → inquiry → financial
          qualification → admission → treatment → revenue.</strong> I call
          that the Golden Thread, and it's the backbone of everything the
          system reasons across.
        </p>
        <p>
          The larger vision behind the Evidence Engine is <Wordmark name="HEOS" />,
          an AI-enabled executive intelligence layer for healthcare
          organizations. Harbor Ridge itself isn't the product; it's the
          testbed I built to put the Evidence Engine through a real,
          controlled test.
        </p>
        <p>
          Before I asked the AI anything, I mapped the organization and
          froze the answers I already knew were hidden inside it. The model
          came first. The evidence came next. The AI came last.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Block D — Video 1 (CEO / Executive Premise) — primary visual weight -->
    <section class="block block--video-primary">
      <div class="measure-display">
        <div class="video-frame video-frame--primary" role="img" aria-label="Video placeholder: Video 1, CEO — Executive Premise">
          <span class="video-frame__play" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          <span class="video-frame__caption">Video 1 — CEO · Executive Premise</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Block E — What It Found -->
    <section class="block">
      <div class="measure">
        <h2 class="section-heading">What It Found</h2>
        <p>
          One result mattered immediately, and it's exactly the kind of
          thing that's easy to miss when marketing and revenue cycle never
          compare notes: <span class="finding-tag">Observed Finding</span> an
          estimated <span class="stat-highlight">$251,000</span> in additional
          collections if out-of-network claims had realized at the same rate
          as in-network ones.
        </p>
        <p>
          That finding was real. But it wasn't the answer to the test I'd
          actually built the system to pass — it was something the AI
          noticed along the way, not one of the specific hidden problems I
          was checking whether it could find.
        </p>
        <p class="inline-link">
          <a href="/evidence-trail">See the full arithmetic behind this number → Evidence</a>
        </p>
        <p>
          When I later tested the Evidence Engine against those specific
          hidden problems, it missed both.
        </p>
        <p>
          I kept both results. The goal was never to manufacture an AI
          success story — it was to build something whose conclusions could
          actually be inspected, tested, and challenged.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Block F — The Real Test -->
    <section class="block">
      <div class="measure">
        <h2 class="section-heading">The Real Test</h2>
        <p>
          A convincing AI demonstration can become misleading when the
          person building it already knows the answer. I ran into that risk
          directly, before the real test even began.
        </p>
        <p>
          While building one of the two hidden test scenarios, a genuine
          validation problem turned up. I had a command ready to search
          across different random seeds until I got a cleaner-looking
          result.
        </p>
        <p class="callout-line">I stopped it before it ran, and fixed the actual problem instead.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Block G — Video 4 (Hiring Manager) — secondary visual weight -->
    <section class="block block--video-secondary">
      <div class="measure">
        <div class="video-frame video-frame--secondary" role="img" aria-label="Video placeholder: Video 4, Hiring Manager — Judgment Under Failure">
          <span class="video-frame__play" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          <span class="video-frame__caption">Video 4 — Hiring Manager · Judgment Under Failure</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Block H — What Happened -->
    <section class="block">
      <div class="measure">
        <h2 class="section-heading">What Happened</h2>
        <p>That same discipline governed the real evaluation. Here's the result:</p>

        <dl class="result-list">
          <div class="result-list__item">
            <dt>Scenario 1</dt>
            <dd>MISS</dd>
          </div>
          <div class="result-list__item">
            <dt>Scenario 2</dt>
            <dd>MISS</dd>
          </div>
          <div class="result-list__item">
            <dt>Healthy Baseline</dt>
            <dd>CLEAN</dd>
          </div>
        </dl>

        <p>
          The Evidence Engine didn't recover either of the two problems I'd
          deliberately hidden for it to find. It also didn't invent a
          problem where none existed — the clean control case stayed clean.
          That's a real, useful signal about restraint, but it doesn't prove
          the system is generally reliable.
        </p>
        <p>
          I could have kept building new versions until one of them passed.
          Instead, I froze the results exactly as they came out, misses
          included, and documented what the evidence actually showed.
        </p>
        <p class="inline-link">
          <a href="/methodology-evaluation">See how the test itself was designed to prevent me from moving the goalposts → Methodology &amp; Evaluation</a>
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Block I — What This Demonstrates -->
    <section class="block">
      <div class="measure">
        <h2 class="section-heading">What This Demonstrates</h2>
        <p>
          For me, the important result wasn't that the system succeeded or
          failed. It was learning exactly where its reasoning was useful,
          where it wasn't, and what the evidence could actually support.
        </p>
        <p>
          That's the kind of analytical judgment I want to bring to
          healthcare growth work: build the system, test it against
          reality, inspect the evidence, and don't claim more than the
          results can support.
        </p>
        <p>
          I also know precisely what would and wouldn't transfer if I built
          something like this for a different organization. That's worth
          exploring on its own.
        </p>
        <p class="inline-link">
          <a href="/portability-lessons">What transfers, and what doesn't → Portability &amp; Lessons</a>
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Block J — Go Deeper -->
    <section class="block block--deeper">
      <div class="measure">
        <h2 class="section-heading">Go Deeper</h2>
        <p>
          If you want to see the reasoning behind any of this rather than
          take my word for it, here's where each piece lives:
        </p>

        <ul class="deeper-links">
          <li><a class="cta" href="/evidence-trail">Evidence — inspect the $251K finding's full arithmetic, live</a></li>
          <li><a class="cta" href="/methodology-evaluation">Methodology &amp; Evaluation — the full technical build and test design</a></li>
          <li><a class="cta" href="/portability-lessons">Portability &amp; Lessons — what would and wouldn't transfer elsewhere</a></li>
          <li><a class="cta" href="/about-contact">About / Contact — who I am, and how to reach me</a></li>
        </ul>

        <p class="deeper-note">
          (Case study, full whitepaper, and GitHub repository are also
          reachable from About/Contact, per Step 2's spec.)
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>

  <style>
    .home {
      max-width: 1100px;
      margin: 0 auto;
      padding: 0 var(--space-4);
    }

    .block {
      padding: var(--space-7) 0;
      border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-slate-border);
    }

    .block:last-of-type {
      border-bottom: none;
    }

    .measure {
      max-width: 70ch;
      margin: 0 auto;
    }

    /* Display container for hero copy and the primary video — wider than
       the 70ch reading measure since these aren't paragraph body text. */
    .measure-display {
      max-width: 900px;
      margin: 0 auto;
    }

    .block--hero {
      padding-top: var(--space-8);
    }

    .hero-headline {
      font-family: var(--font-heading);
      font-weight: 700;
      font-size: var(--text-hero);
      line-height: var(--line-height-heading);
      margin: 0 0 var(--space-4) 0;
    }

    .lead {
      font-size: var(--text-lead);
      color: var(--color-slate-text-secondary);
      margin: 0;
    }

    .section-heading {
      font-family: var(--font-heading);
      font-weight: 700;
      font-size: var(--text-h2);
      line-height: var(--line-height-heading-sub);
      margin: 0 0 var(--space-4) 0;
    }

    .block p {
      margin: 0 0 var(--space-4) 0;
    }

    .block p:last-child {
      margin-bottom: 0;
    }

    .inline-link {
      font-size: var(--text-small);
    }

    .inline-link a {
      color: var(--color-sovereign-navy);
      text-decoration: underline;
    }

    .inline-link a:hover {
      color: var(--color-sovereign-navy);
    }

    .callout-line {
      font-family: var(--font-heading);
      font-weight: 600;
      font-size: var(--text-lead);
      line-height: var(--line-height-heading);
    }

    .finding-tag {
      display: inline-block;
      font-family: var(--font-heading);
      font-weight: 600;
      font-size: var(--text-small);
      text-transform: uppercase;
      letter-spacing: 0.05em;
      color: var(--color-slate-text-secondary);
      border: 1px solid var(--color-slate-border);
      padding: var(--space-1) var(--space-2);
      margin-right: var(--space-2);
      white-space: nowrap;
    }

    /* Video placeholders — occupied space at the approved aspect ratio,
       not a fabricated thumbnail image. Primary (Block D) spans the full
       measure; secondary (Block G) is deliberately narrower and lower in
       the page, per Step 2's "unequal visual weight" requirement. */
    .video-frame {
      position: relative;
      width: 100%;
      aspect-ratio: 16 / 9;
      background: var(--color-sovereign-navy);
      color: var(--color-white);
      display: flex;
      flex-direction: column;
      align-items: center;
      justify-content: center;
      gap: var(--space-3);
    }

    .video-frame__play {
      display: inline-block;
      width: 0;
      height: 0;
      border-top: 14px solid transparent;
      border-bottom: 14px solid transparent;
      border-left: 22px solid var(--color-white);
      margin-left: 6px;
    }

    .video-frame--primary .video-frame__play {
      border-top-width: 20px;
      border-bottom-width: 20px;
      border-left-width: 32px;
    }

    .video-frame__caption {
      font-family: var(--font-heading);
      font-weight: 600;
      font-size: var(--text-small);
      letter-spacing: 0.03em;
    }

    .block--video-secondary .measure {
      max-width: 46ch;
    }

    .result-list {
      display: flex;
      flex-direction: column;
      gap: var(--space-2);
      margin: 0 0 var(--space-4) 0;
      padding: 0;
    }

    .result-list__item {
      display: flex;
      align-items: baseline;
      justify-content: space-between;
      gap: var(--space-4);
      background: var(--color-steel-gray-surface);
      border: 1px solid var(--color-slate-border);
      padding: var(--space-3) var(--space-4);
    }

    .result-list__item dt {
      font-family: var(--font-heading);
      font-weight: 600;
      font-size: var(--text-small);
      color: var(--color-slate-text-secondary);
    }

    .result-list__item dd {
      margin: 0;
      font-family: var(--font-heading);
      font-weight: 700;
      font-size: var(--text-h3);
      letter-spacing: 0.03em;
    }

    .deeper-links {
      list-style: none;
      margin: 0 0 var(--space-4) 0;
      padding: 0;
      display: grid;
      grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
      gap: var(--space-3);
    }

    .deeper-links .cta {
      width: 100%;
      height: 100%;
      text-align: left;
      justify-content: flex-start;
      white-space: normal;
      line-height: var(--line-height-body);
    }

    .deeper-note {
      font-size: var(--text-small);
      color: var(--color-slate-text-secondary);
      font-style: italic;
      margin: 0;
    }

    @media (max-width: 900px) {
      .hero-headline {
        font-size: var(--text-h1);
      }
    }

    @media (max-width: 640px) {
      .block {
        padding: var(--space-6) 0;
      }

      .deeper-links {
        grid-template-columns: 1fr;
      }

      .result-list__item {
        flex-direction: column;
        gap: var(--space-1);
      }
    }
  </style>
</Layout>
